12 hidden features of my japanese house  full house tour video here point number  one roof tiles one of the main features  of the house  is this central courtyard that the house  wraps around there are these roof tiles  traditional japanese roof tiles in this  corner of the courtyard and what's  special about these is that these were  actually made by my wife's grandfather  he made these for a living my wife and i  had seen people use roof tiles in this  way sort of recycling them to create  this zen aesthetic in architecture we  wanted to do the same thing while also  sort of you know injecting her family's  dna into the house point number two  hidden full-length mirror so right now  we're in the gangkong of the house the  entryway to the house but this is  actually the guest gengong we don't keep  our shoes here such that when guests  come over they can smoothly come up into  the house and not have to walk over our  shoes and other other things we keep all  of those things  in this room this is the family gang and  when we come to the house we use this  space but what's special about this  space is that on the other side of this  door  hidden inside of the door itself is this  full-length mirror you can check your  appearance before you leave and this  also enables us to  not then need  one of these standing full-length  mirrors that takes up a bunch of space  somewhere else in the house point number  three  so we have a roomba and the reason for  that is because except for the guest  room all the floor space of this house  is on the first floor of the house which  is the type of house in which a roomba  really shines we knew that we wanted one  early on but we had also heard a bunch  of horror stories from people that  bought these five or six hundred dollar  robot vacuum cleaners but then ended up  not really using them because when  you're not using them they've got these  charging stations and the room by itself  is actually surprisingly large and they  end up taking like  up an entire corner like this awkward  section of your living room then you  have to like step over it and you bump  into it and it ends up being more of a  hassle than it actually is but because  we knew we wanted one early on what our  architect suggested was this we've got  this little storage closet right here  and he said we could cut out just the  bottom of that closet put an outlet in  there for the charging station you never  have to see it but then you would still  get all the benefits of the roomba and  when it's all done it just goes back  home by itself point number four hidden  cable drawer so one of the things that i  love so much about this architect is how  he hides things as a youtuber i have a  bunch of  you know usb cables hdmi cables audio  cables and taking a cue from the uh  taking a clue taking a cue from the  architect i installed this drawer  under this desk itself so i keep all you  know my camera trinkets and stuff like  that that uh  i use maybe once or twice a year and  can't really throw away they're all  under there and they're out of sight and  out of mind when i'm not using them so  point number five is office lighting  we're currently in my office and in the  original tour video i talked a little  bit about how these  side lights and these light rails along  the ceiling enabled me to get rid of the  giant light and tripod that i had in my  previous office that took up like half  my room what i didn't talk about in that  video is how  you can actually adjust the temperature  and  brightness of all these lights as well  so i've got this little  switcher over here and i can press these  buttons to  make these lights dimmer  or  change the temperature on these and  oftentimes what i do at night is  i'll turn everything except except these  side lights off and it kind of gets me  in that mood before bed if i've got one  of my youtube buddies here we can use  this flexibility to shoot another video  in a different part of the office okay  so point number six is cable management  this is something that i'm  very very pleased with how it turned out  i've got a fairly intense setup when it  comes to cables but you shouldn't be  able to see  most of them and the main reason for  that is because although  most outlets are at that height i  believe this is the standard height for  an outlet in japan about 10 or so  centimeters off the ground the height of  the two outlets that are under this desk  is different from that of all the other  outlets in the house you can't really  see them  unless you either duck down or the desk  is very very high so maybe now you can  see the ethernet cord if you kind of  squat down here  you can see what's  actually going on in here up from above  when i'm working i can't see almost any  of those cables so i'm really happy with  how this turned out point number seven  is hidden storage so there's hidden  storage somewhere in this room  where is it  that's right it's under the tatami mat  so when you move or replace a tatami mat  you use a sharp kind of needle-like  thing like this  to stab the side of it rather than  making a big scratch along the top of it  you want to kind of stab the side like  that and then you can lift it up  here in my office  right here  there's a storage space  a great place for again just storing  various things that i don't want to  think about but can't throw away okay so  we're currently in the kitchen and this  behind me is the pantry and there's a  door here  for when you want to  block off the pantry my wife has a  little desk down here so if she wants  some personal time and wants to you know  get some refuge from me but point number  eight is actually  this  on the other side of the door frame  there's this chalkboard that pops out  and the whole purpose of this is it  gives my wife a way to write something  down quickly when she's in the middle of  cooking something and or to reference  what she's written down previously even  when she has greasy hands you know you  don't want to take out your expensive  thousand dollar iphone if you've got  grease all over your hands or if you've  got flour all over your hands or  something like that but if it's chalk  you know there's a little piece of chalk  that we keep over here and she can just  write things down quickly and then again  look back and forth between the kitchen  and right here in the middle of cooking  really just kind of follows the  out of sight out of mind design language  of the house in the original tour video  i talked about  these these shogi japanese paper doors  that come out of the walls great way to  change the atmosphere of the room but  it's also highly practical because it  allows us to block out the extremely  intense japanese summer sun it gets so  hot in japan during the summer that i i  shaved my head i'm balding that's  another reason but it's hot we've got  these paper doors for  this window and then that much larger  window over there maybe you can get a  shot of that real quick  but then point number nine is actually  this these electric roll screens for the  windows up above same basic idea this  gives us a practical easy way to block  out that intense summer sun without  overworking our ac during the summer we  could have gone with um you know  a string as well but we were a little  worried about having we've got two  daughters and two small daughters that  are constantly you know running along  this kind of area of the house and we  were thinking you know they might get  like kind of strung up in in that or get  like an arm caught on that then it could  yank the entire thing down from the  ceiling so we went electric and uh it  just also again kind of cleans up the  space much more there's no  there aren't these two big strings that  go all the way from the ceiling all the  way to the floor currently in the  family closet area one of the things  that we didn't talk about in the  original tour video is  this 